{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210150,"nonce":"rkeCGm0FxG56GAp8JszplYwKgZw=","target":"8f2371453942aafb6c78e4c358e82625473d22370e95651fb214b2952f5c35f4","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0006 is titled \"the reconciler is mandatory, **not\noptional**\", and its rationale is that without it oto \"will eventually claim an\nalert resolved when it was merely silenced — which destroys the system-of-record\nclaim\". The schema and the API contradict it:\n`alert_sources.reconcile_enabled boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT true`,\n`internal/sources/repository/sources.go:221` gates the due-list on\n`AND s.reconcile_enabled`, and it is a settable contract field\n(`internal/sources/api/dto.go:52,214,246`; `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:6701`). The\naudit verified it live: `PATCH /api/v1/sources/{id} {\"reconcile_enabled\":false}`\n→ **200, persisted** (`review-conformance.md`, ADR 0006 row).\n\n**Why it matters.** Suppression is only learnable from Alertmanager's API v2 —\nAlertmanager drops suppressed alerts before the webhook fires. With reconciliation\noff, an alert that a colleague silenced keeps showing as firing and then gets\nexpired by the reaper with `reason=timeout`, so oto records a resolution that\nnever happened. One PATCH, made by any org member for a plausible reason (\"it is\nnoisy\"), turns the history from a record into a fiction, permanently and with no\nwarning anywhere in the UI.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/*` (`alert_sources.reconcile_enabled`),\n`internal/sources/repository/sources.go:221`, `internal/sources/api/dto.go`,\n`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:6701`, `docs/adr/0006-reconciler-is-mandatory.md`.\n\n**Done when.** Either the field is removed from the write path (interval stays\ntunable, on/off does not) and the column is dropped or pinned true; or ADR 0006 is\namended with the reasoning for making it optional and the UI states plainly, on\nthe source and on every alert from it, that resolution data from that source is\nnot trustworthy. Silently keeping both is the one outcome that must not stand.","files":null}]}